---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Crumbs from "../components/Crumbs.astro";
import { company, fullAddress, nap } from "../data/company";
import { breadcrumbSchema, businessSchema, graph } from "../lib/schema";

const path = "/about";
const title = "About us";
const description =
  "Electrotech Electrical Services is a Bedford electrician at 5 Miller Road, MK42 9FS. Customers name Stuart. Call 01234 325435.";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "About", path },
];
const jsonLd = graph([
  businessSchema(Astro.site),
  breadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, crumbs),
  {
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    name: title,
    url: new URL(path, Astro.site).href,
    description,
    about: { "@id": new URL("/#business", Astro.site).href },
  },
]);
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Crumbs items={crumbs} />
      <p class="eyebrow">About us</p>
      <h1>A Bedford electrician, based on Miller Road</h1>
      <p>
        {company.legalName} is listed at {fullAddress()}. The phone number on that Google listing is {company.phoneDisplay}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        Customers who have written about the work name Stuart as the electrician they dealt with.
        DK Brawn’s Google review, Andy Christoforou, Shelly Martin-Smith, Robert Harris, John Parrott,
        and Alan Crompton all use that name. We have not published a surname.
      </p>
      <p>
        Electrotech has described itself as a firm that stands on the standard of the work and on how customers
        are treated. The published line is that a new build, a rewire, and a single light or socket should all
        get the same care.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finished work is said to come with a certificate to BS 7671, the British Standard for electrical
        installations. For home work that must meet Part P of the Building Regulations, Electrotech has said
        a building notice is sent within 30 days. Keep that paper. Solicitors ask for it when a house is sold.
      </p>

      <h2>What an older website said about registration</h2>
      <div class="note">
        <p>
          Electrotech’s website, in pages saved from 2010 and 2014, said the firm was an NICEIC domestic
          installer and an NICEIC approved contractor. It also said the firm held City &amp; Guilds qualifications,
          public liability insurance of £2 million, TrustMark, and membership of the Bedford Borough Trading
          Standards approved scheme.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Google listing checked on {company.checkedOn} does not show those badges, and it is not a claimed
          listing. Ask for current proof of insurance, and for any registration your insurer or your buyer’s
          solicitor wants to see, before work starts.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Addresses we have published</h2>
      <p>
        The live Google listing is {nap}. An earlier Electrotech website also gave
        {company.earlierStreet}, {company.earlierLocality}, {company.earlierPostcode}, with the phone
        01234 743979. Use {company.phoneDisplay}, which is the number on the current listing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Google profile has no opening hours and no website link. It has one review and no work photos.
        The photo control opens a street view near Miller Road. That picture shows neighbouring houses, so
        it is not reprinted here. The map on the contact page is the listing itself.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/contact">Contact Electrotech</a> or <a href="/reviews">read the reviews</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
